WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.273
Iterators, references, pointers, spans, and views are lifetime-sensitive even before concurrency enters the picture.

2
00:00:05.273 --> 00:00:07.733
A container operation can invalidate an iterator.

3
00:00:07.733 --> 00:00:09.842
A vector reallocation can move elements.

4
00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:13.006
An erase can destroy the object behind a reference.

5
00:00:13.006 --> 00:00:21.442
In concurrent code, these risks become easier to trigger because the caller holding the access and the caller mutating the container may be running

6
00:00:21.442 --> 00:00:22.848
at the same time.

7
00:00:22.848 --> 00:00:30.230
A method that returns internal access can accidentally require every caller to understand the container's invalidation and locking rules perfectly.

8
00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.394
Safer APIs try to prevent stale access by construction.

9
00:00:33.394 --> 00:00:37.612
One option is to return an owned copy of the requested data.

10
00:00:37.612 --> 00:00:42.533
Another is to return an immutable snapshot that represents a point-in-time view.

11
00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:51.673
A third is to return a scoped access object that keeps a lock or lifetime token associated with the data until the access object is destroyed.

12
00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:59.055
The right choice depends on cost, freshness, and lifetime needs, but the API should make the rule obvious in normal use.

13
00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:02.921
Snapshot semantics are especially useful when a caller needs to iterate.

14
00:01:02.921 --> 00:01:11.709
Instead of holding a lock while arbitrary caller code walks internal state, the structure can build a list of stable values or handles and release

15
00:01:11.709 --> 00:01:14.170
the lock before the caller processes them.

16
00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:17.685
That reduces lock duration and avoids exposing internal mutation details.

17
00:01:17.685 --> 00:01:21.552
When snapshots are expensive, document the tradeoff and measure it honestly.

18
00:01:21.552 --> 00:01:28.582
The key defensive principle is that comments alone should not be the only thing preventing stale iterator or reference use.

19
00:01:28.582 --> 00:01:32.800
Prefer return types and access patterns that guide callers toward correct behavior.
